Recognize taps with Flutter's constants and double-tap rules, and drive the recognizer by the executor's clock.

What: adds `gestures/constants.rs` (public as `gpui::constants`), a port of Flutter's constants.dart @ 3.50.1 with
Flutter's names (`kTouchSlop`, `kDoubleTapTimeout`, ...), the one copy GPUI's recognizers and Parallax's platform
behaviour table read. `GestureTuning::default()` holds those constants (the same on every platform): `touch_slop`
kTouchSlop 18 px (was 8), `multi_tap_interval` kDoubleTapTimeout 300 ms (was 400), `multi_tap_slop` kDoubleTapSlop 100
px (was 16), the new `multi_tap_min_interval` kDoubleTapMinTime 40 ms, `long_press_duration` kLongPressTimeout 500 ms,
`min_fling_velocity` kMinFlingVelocity 50 px/s; the fling ceiling is kMaxFlingVelocity (was a private copy of it). A
tap's click count follows `DoubleTapGestureRecognizer`: it continues the sequence when the new touch starts less than
the interval after the previous tap's up, within the slop of where the previous tap started, and at least the min
interval after the previous tap started; otherwise it starts a new sequence at 1. GPUI kept counting past 2 (triple
click) and still does. `TouchGestureRecognizer::handle_event`, `pending_long_press` and `tick_momentum` take `now`
from the window's `cx.background_executor().now()` instead of `Instant::now()`. `Window::set_gesture_tuning`
(`#[cfg(test)]`) lets a test pass its own tuning.

Why: Flutter's recognizer tests, ported as table rows (`gestures/recognizer_tests.rs`, below), fail on GPUI's tuning
and tap counting: 5 of 44 rows. An 8 px slop makes a 17 px wiggle a pan ("Short distance does not cancel tap") and
starts a pan at 14 px ("Should recognize pan"); with no minimum interval, a touch screen's split touch counts as a
double tap ("Should not recognize two over-rapid taps", "Over-rapid taps resets double tap…", "No duplicate tap
events"). Flutter raised its slop from 8 to 18 after complaints that targets were too hard to hit. The wall clock made
the recognizer impossible to drive from GPUI's test platform (the executor docs: "Calling this instead of
`std::time::Instant::now` allows the use of fake timers in tests"); the long-press timer already ran on the executor,
so its deadline and its timer now read the same clock. 0003's pointer-source test relied on wall time passing between
dispatches for its fling; it now advances the test clock 8 ms per input.

Test: `gestures::recognizer_tests::flutter_recognizer_tests_pass`, 44 rows from tap_test.dart (12),
double_tap_test.dart (10), long_press_test.dart (11) and drag_test.dart (11). Each row is a recorded touch trace
(time, touch id, phase, position) replayed into a test window, Flutter's tuning passed explicitly, and the gestures
the window dispatched with their times (tap with click count, scroll steps, claimed long press and touch drag). Arena,
button, device-kind, multi-pointer and velocity cases are not rows: docs/gesture-recognizers.md lists them.

Ported code: constants.dart (`gestures/constants.rs`, whole), the tap-count rule (multitap.dart), and the test rows
(the four test files). Copyright 2014 The Flutter Authors, BSD-3-Clause.

Size: ~1300 lines, ~790 of them the new test table and ~120 constants.rs; the recognizer and window changes are ~170,
most of it rustfmt rewrapping call sites that gained the `now` argument.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.


